---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import RecentWork from "../../components/RecentWork.astro";

import HubspotPartner from "../../assets/hubspot.svg";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="HubSpot Website Design &amp; Development | Kelp"
  description="HubSpot website design, migration, content modules, and connected experiences built around your sales and marketing workflows."
  darkHeader={true}
>
  <article>
    <SimpleHero
      title="HubSpot"
      description="We build scalable, user-friendly HubSpot websites, themes, and modules designed for marketing teams to actually use — not fight against. As official HubSpot Solutions Partners and multi-award-winning theme developers, we know the CMS inside and out."
      dark={true}
    />

    <section class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <div class="platform">
        <div class="platform-logo">
          <HubspotPartner />
        </div>

        <div class="platform-text">
          <p>
            HubSpot CMS is powerful&thinsp;—&thinsp;but only when it’s built the
            right way. We focus on creating flexible, marketer-friendly themes,
            modules, and landing pages that empower your team instead of slowing
            them down.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our award-winning HubSpot themes and modules are used by hundreds of
            companies and were recognized in HubSpot’s official CMS Themes
            Challenge. On top of HubSpot CMS Themes we've done extensive work in
            HubSpot's HubDB tables, custom serverless functions to sync data,
            and custom workflow webhooks to trigger said serveless functions
            based on specific events.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="service-body">
      <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
        <h2>Where Teams Get Stuck</h2>
        <p>
          Marketing teams can outgrow a HubSpot site when every page requires
          developer help or when forms and workflows are hard to connect to the
          real sales process. A migration can also preserve old content problems
          if the new structure is not planned.
        </p>
        <h2>How We Fix It</h2>
        <p>
          We map the content model, editor workflow, forms, automation, and data
          handoffs before building themes or modules. Where the process requires
          more, Kelp's published HubSpot capabilities include HubDB, serverless
          functions, and workflow webhooks. We keep the editor experience usable
          for the team that will own it.
        </p>
        <h2>Plan the Migration and the Content</h2>
        <p>
          We review existing pages, define the structure, and design reusable
          templates and modules that a team can maintain. Forms, calls to
          action, and handoffs should support a clear next step for the visitor
          and a useful record for the team.
        </p>
        <h2>Make Complex Experiences Feel Connected</h2>
        <p>
          Kelp's <a href="/work/hsr/">HS&amp;R</a> project combined content strategy, copy, design, and
          HubSpot website migration. For <a href="/work/artisan-talent/">Artisan Talent</a>, Kelp worked across
          HubSpot, WordPress, and a custom jobs application so applicants
          encountered a consistent experience. Discuss your HubSpot site.
        </p>
        <p>
          Kelp's current HubSpot platform work also covers custom themes and
          modules, HubDB, serverless functions, and workflow webhooks when a
          standard page or form cannot support the process. We scope those
          connections around the team's data and approval needs.
        </p>
        <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
        <p>
          A HubSpot website can support more than publishing pages. Forms, CRM
          data, segmentation, and marketing workflows can connect the visitor
          experience to the follow-up that happens after a lead arrives. We map
          that handoff before building templates or migrating content. Editors
          also need reusable modules and clear governance so the website remains
          consistent as campaigns and teams grow.
        </p>
        <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>HubSpot site strategy and migration</li>
          <li>Custom themes, modules, and landing pages</li>
          <li>Forms, HubDB, and workflow connections</li>
          <li>Editor documentation and launch support</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
        <p>
          Bring the current portal, page inventory, sales process, and the tasks
          marketers cannot complete easily today. We can define the content and
          technical migration together.
        </p>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <h3>Can HubSpot connect to other platforms?</h3>
        <p>
          Often, yes. The approach depends on the APIs, data ownership, and
          workflow. We scope the connection before choosing a native feature or
          custom integration.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
            Talk Through Your Project
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <RecentWork
    tag="89"
    limit={10}
    heading="HubSpot Projects"
    description="<p>Here’s a look at some of the HubSpot builds we’ve launched — crafted to make content management and campaign execution seamless.</p>"
  />

  <FeaturedArticles
    category="12"
    heading="Our Articles about HubSpot"
    limit={3}
  />
</Layout>

<style lang="scss">
  .platform {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 2rem 6rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    padding: 4rem 0;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--teal);

    &:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    &-logo {
      flex: 0 1 220px;
      text-align: center;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 2rem;
      order: 2;
      svg {
        width: 100%;
        height: auto;
      }
      @media (max-width: 767px) {
        margin: auto;
      }
    }

    &-text {
      flex: 1 1 500px;
    }
  }
</style>
